---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Section from "./Section.astro";
import { t, langPath, type Lang } from "../i18n/ui";
import { demoAllows } from "../lib/site.js";
import { ORG, ATTORNEY, show } from "../lib/org";
import { graph, orgSchema, attorneySchema, breadcrumbSchema } from "../lib/seo";

/* camimlaw.com/about, then Campos Muños Law's biography of him (camulaw.com,
   team.members.juan.bio) minus the one paragraph that places his practice in
   Louisiana (see docs/sources/omissions.json), then record values. */
interface Props { lang: Lang }
const { lang } = Astro.props;
const _ = t(lang);
const es = lang === "es";
const admissions = show(ATTORNEY.admissions) ?? [];
const education = show(ATTORNEY.education) ?? [];
const memberships = show(ATTORNEY.memberships) ?? [];
const portrait = ATTORNEY.recent.value; // the newer café photograph from the sister site, cropped to him
const path = langPath(lang, "juan-campos");
const alternates = { es: "/juan-campos/", en: "/en/juan-campos/" };

const camim = es
  ? {
      h: "Soy un abogado de inmigración con sede en Orlando",
      p: [
        "Como abogado de inmigración con experiencia, me dedico a brindar servicios legales excepcionales a personas y familias necesitadas. Ya sea que esté buscando ayuda con visas, tarjetas verdes u otros asuntos de inmigración, tengo el conocimiento y la experiencia para guiarlo a través del complejo proceso.",
        "Mi equipo y yo entendemos la importancia de su viaje de inmigración y el impacto que puede tener en su vida. Estamos aquí para apoyarlo en cada paso del camino, ofreciéndole soluciones personalizadas adaptadas a sus circunstancias únicas. Con un profundo conocimiento de las leyes y regulaciones de inmigración, me comprometo a lograr el mejor resultado posible para nuestros clientes. Me esfuerzo por brindar representación legal profesional, eficiente y compasiva, asegurando que sus derechos estén protegidos y que su voz sea escuchada.",
      ],
    }
  : null;
const bio = (es
  ? [
      "El derecho migratorio no es solo la profesión de Juan Campos-Gutiérrez — es algo personal. Nacido en Venezuela, aporta más de dos décadas de experiencia legal a su práctica, junto con una comprensión de primera mano de lo que significa empezar de nuevo en un país distinto.",
      "Fue durante sus estudios de posgrado en los Estados Unidos, mientras cursaba una Maestría en Derecho Comparado (LL.M.) en la Universidad de Florida, que Juan encontró el trabajo para el cual estaba destinado. El derecho de inmigración y nacionalidad resonó en él de una manera que nada más lo había hecho, y tomó la decisión de reconstruir su carrera en torno a esa vocación.",
      "Antes de fundar su propia práctica, Juan se desempeñó como Abogado de Caridades Católicas en New Orleans, donde representó a la población inmigrante principalmente de Centroamérica — defendiendo sus derechos en algunos de los procesos más delicados y complejos del sistema migratorio.",
      "Juan está admitido para ejercer la abogacía ante la Corte Suprema del Estado de Luisiana y es miembro de la American Immigration Lawyers Association (AILA). Ofrece servicios legales en todas las áreas del derecho de inmigración y nacionalidad, y mantiene una presencia activa en redes sociales, donde comparte orientación e información para mantener informadas a las comunidades inmigrantes.",
      "A lo largo de su carrera, Juan ha representado con éxito a cientos de clientes a nivel administrativo, compareciendo ante las Cortes de Inmigración, el Servicio de Ciudadanía e Inmigración de los Estados Unidos (USCIS) y el Departamento de Estado — representando clientes en distintas jurisdicciones a nivel nacional.",
      "Juan conoce a fondo el proceso migratorio, y aporta una pasión genuina a cada caso que asume. Su práctica abarca la mayoría de las áreas del derecho de inmigración, pero en el fondo, se trata de las personas: construir relaciones reales con sus clientes y acompañar a inmigrantes, minorías y personas en situación de desventaja. Proteger los derechos de los inmigrantes se ha convertido en algo más que una profesión para Juan — se ha convertido en su misión. Como inmigrante él mismo, comprende de primera mano las preocupaciones de sus clientes, y se esfuerza por explicar las complejidades del derecho migratorio de una manera sencilla, clara y fácil de entender.",
    ]
  : [
      "Immigration law isn't just Juan's profession — it's personal. Born in Venezuela, he brings more than two decades of legal experience to his practice, along with a firsthand understanding of what it means to start over in a new country.",
      "It was during his graduate studies in the United States, while earning a Master of Comparative Law (LL.M.) from the University of Florida, that Juan found the work he was meant to do. Immigration and nationality law resonated with him in a way nothing else had, and he made the decision to rebuild his career around it.",
      "Before opening his own practice, Juan served as a Staff Attorney with Catholic Charities Archdiocese of New Orleans, where he represented unaccompanied children — defending their rights in some of the most sensitive and complex proceedings in the immigration system.",
      "Juan is admitted to practice law before the Louisiana Supreme Court and is a member of the American Immigration Lawyers Association (AILA). He provides legal services across the full scope of Immigration and Nationality Law, and is active on social media, where he shares guidance and updates to help immigrant communities stay informed.",
      "Over the course of his career, Juan has successfully represented hundreds of clients at the administrative level, appearing before the Immigration Courts, U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services, and the U.S. Department of State — representing clients across jurisdictions nationwide.",
      "Juan is intimately familiar with the immigration process, and he brings genuine passion to every case he takes on. His practice spans most areas of immigration law, but at its core, it's about people: building real relationships with his clients and standing beside immigrants, minorities, and those facing disadvantage. Protecting immigrants' rights has become more than a profession for Juan — it's become his mission. As an immigrant himself, he understands his clients' concerns firsthand, and works to explain the complexities of immigration law in a way that's simple, clear, and easy to understand.",
    ]);
/* camimlaw.com has a first-person line for the header; Campos Munos Law's
   English does not, so the English header promotes the biography's own opening
   paragraph and the biography below starts at the second. Placement, not new
   words, and the paragraph is not left in both places. */
const lead = camim?.h ?? bio[0];
const bioBody = camim ? bio : bio.slice(1);

const crumbs = [{ name: _("nav.home"), url: langPath(lang) }, { name: _("nav.attorney"), url: path }];
const schema = graph([orgSchema(), attorneySchema(lang), breadcrumbSchema(crumbs)]);
const title = `${ATTORNEY.displayName.value}, ${ATTORNEY.credentialSuffix.value} — ${ORG.name.value}`;
---
<Base lang={lang} title={title} description={es ? camim!.h : bio[0]} path={path} alternates={alternates} schema={schema}>
  <nav class="crumbs wrap-wide">
    <ol>{crumbs.map((c, i) => <li>{i < crumbs.length - 1 ? <a href={c.url}>{c.name}</a> : <span aria-current="page">{c.name}</span>}</li>)}</ol>
  </nav>

  <section class="ahead" data-surface="paper">
   <div class="wrap-wide ahead-inner">
    <div class="ahead-text">
      <p class="eyebrow">{_("nav.attorney")}</p>
      <h1 class="ahead-name" data-split-lines="hero">{ATTORNEY.displayName.value}, {ATTORNEY.credentialSuffix.value}</h1>
      <p class="ahead-lead">{lead}</p>
      {/* The record: admissions, degrees, memberships. It used to be a section
          of its own further down with an empty left margin beside it, which
          gave the page a thin band and left this column with nothing under his
          name to balance the photograph. On a biography page it belongs here. */}
      <ul class="ahead-creds">
        {admissions.map((a) => <li>{a}</li>)}
        {education.map((e) => <li>{e.degree[lang]}, {e.school}, <span class="apparatus">{e.year}</span></li>)}
        {memberships.map((m) => <li>{m}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
    <figure class="ahead-figure" data-reveal="portrait">
      <img src={portrait.src} width={portrait.width} height={portrait.height} alt={`${ATTORNEY.displayName.value}, ${ATTORNEY.credentialSuffix.value}`} loading="eager" decoding="async" />
    </figure>
   </div>
  </section>

  {camim && (
    <Section surface="paper" wide class="tight">
      <div class="reading">
        <div class="prose">{camim.p.map((t) => <p>{t}</p>)}</div>
      </div>
    </Section>
  )}

  <Section surface="paper-2" wide class="tight">
    <div class="reading">
      <div class="prose">{bioBody.map((t) => <p>{t}</p>)}</div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="paper-2" wide>
    <div class="editorial">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">{_("home.questions")}</p></div>
      <div>
        <p class="lead">{_("contact.subtitle")}</p>
        <p>{demoAllows("consulta")
          ? <a class="btn btn-primary" href={langPath(lang, "consulta")}>{_("cta.book")}</a>
          : <a class="btn btn-primary" href={`tel:${ORG.phone.value}`}>{_("cta.call")} {ORG.phoneDisplay.value}</a>}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>
</Base>

<style>
  /* One reading column, at the page's own left edge. These two sections sat
     in the editorial grid with an empty 12rem label column and a 64px gap
     beside them, so the prose began 376px in while the header above it began
     at the gutter — two left edges and a margin holding nothing. The grid is
     for sections that have a label; these do not. */
  .reading { max-width: var(--container-m-read); }
  /* The page is one article, not a stack of separate sections. At the site's
     --section-y of 160px top and bottom there were 264px between his
     credentials and the first sentence about him, and another 264 before the
     biography — a third of the page was the gaps between its own paragraphs. */
  :global(.section.tight) { padding-block: clamp(44px, 4.4vw, 72px); }

  /* No bottom padding: the section below carries its own top padding and it
     is the same surface, so both together read as one hole rather than as a
     seam between two things. */
  .ahead { padding-block: var(--spacing-s6) 0; }
  .ahead-inner { display: grid; gap: var(--spacing-s7); }
  .ahead-text { display: grid; gap: var(--spacing-s5); align-content: start; min-width: 0; }
  /* The same display rank as the home page's introduction, so the two pages
     of the preview are one typographic system rather than two. */
  .ahead-name {
    margin: 0; font-family: var(--font-serif); font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(34px, 21px + 3.1vw, 62px);
    line-height: 1.04; letter-spacing: -0.025em; text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .ahead-lead { margin: 0; font-size: var(--text-lead); line-height: 1.58; max-width: var(--container-m-lead); }
  .ahead-creds {
    list-style: none; margin: var(--spacing-s2) 0 0; padding: 0;
    display: grid; max-width: var(--container-m-read);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--surface-line);
  }
  .ahead-creds li { padding-block: var(--spacing-s4); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--surface-line); }

  .ahead-figure { margin: 0; }
  /* Whole, at its own proportions. The 4/5 crop this used to carry cut through
     the chair and his shin to fit a frame the photograph is not shaped like. */
  .ahead-figure img {
    display: block; width: 100%; height: auto;
    border-radius: var(--radius-md); background: var(--color-paper-2);
  }
  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .ahead-inner {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 0.62fr);
      gap: clamp(48px, 5vw, 96px);
      align-items: start;
    }
    .ahead-figure { justify-self: end; width: 100%; max-width: 400px; }
  }
  /* On a phone the photograph is the first thing, then his name under it —
     but as a band, not a snapshot. Capped by height with `width: auto` it came
     out 305px wide inside a 412px screen, floating between two margins, and it
     filled the whole first screen so his name was below the fold on the page
     that exists to say who he is. It fills the column now and is short enough
     that the name and the summary line arrive with it. */
  @media (max-width: 899px) {
    .ahead-inner { grid-template-areas: "fig" "text"; gap: var(--spacing-s6); }
    .ahead-figure { grid-area: fig; }
    .ahead-text { grid-area: text; }
    .ahead-figure img {
      width: 100%; height: clamp(230px, 40vh, 340px);
      object-fit: cover; object-position: 50% 34%;
    }
  }
</style>
